{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-37650",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To find the roots of the polynomial, we must solve the equation $p(x)=0,$ that is\n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of ${x}^{2}.$ We can factor out the ${x}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-7x-15) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $2{x}^{2}-7x-15$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-7x-15) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(2x+3)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x=0,$ $x=-\\frac{3}{2},$ and $x=5.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
